<script>
' Real, live HTTP GET request/endpoint component — see GRAMMAR.md's "Requests" section. Driven by
' RequestDemoScreen.thr the same way TaskDemoScreen.thr drives SlowTask.thr: taskManager.run(task)
' plus the caller's own observeFieldScoped("result"/"error", ...), the only consumption style
' implemented so far (see docs/features.md for the planned taskManager.onResult(...) promise-style
' sugar). Targets https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts — a free, HTTPS, no-auth-required
' public REST test API, no real backend of this app's own. Confirmed live end to end on a real
' device — see findings/requests-runtime.md.
'
' method/url are the only STATIC config here — everything that varies per call (the `userId`
' query filter) goes through `buildRequest(requestData)` below instead, reading whatever the
' caller passed via `task.callFunc("prepareRequest", requestData)` before `taskManager.run(task)`.
' This is the actual answer to "how do I parameterize a request" — `request Http {}`'s own config
' literal can only ever hold compile-time-fixed values; anything that depends on a runtime value
' (a caller-supplied id, a user's typed search text, a page number) has to flow through this hook
' instead.
request Http {
  method: "GET",
  url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts"
}

' `buildRequest` runs on the RENDER thread, inside the compiler-generated `prepareRequest(...)` —
' called by the caller (RequestDemoScreen.thr) via `task.callFunc("prepareRequest", requestData)`
' BEFORE `taskManager.run(task)` ever starts this Task's own background thread — deliberately, not
' inside `ft_runRequest()` (the actual Task-thread work function) the way phase 1 first shipped it.
' Reason: if `buildRequest`'s own body ever read something outside this node — `store`/`theme`/
' `m.global.*`/another node's field — doing that from a Task's background thread triggers a real
' Roku *rendezvous* (the background thread blocks, waiting on the render thread to service that
' foreign-node access). Resolving the whole request BEFORE the thread even exists sidesteps this
' entirely — see GRAMMAR.md's "Requests" section and findings/requests-runtime.md for the full rationale.
' jsonplaceholder's /posts endpoint supports ?userId=<n> filtering, so a different
' `requestData.userId` per call visibly changes the result (10 posts per user, 100 total) — a
' concrete way to prove the query string is actually per-call, not baked in at compile time.
'
' The `"userId"` key here is QUOTED, deliberately — not a style choice. A bareword key
' (`{ userId: ... }`) gets case-folded to `userid` by BrightScript itself the moment this object
' literal is actually built at runtime (confirmed live: the unquoted form silently sent
' `?userid=1`, which jsonplaceholder's case-sensitive filter ignored, returning all 100 posts
' instead of 10). `request Http {}`'s own STATIC config literal above doesn't have this trap —
' the compiler always re-prints every static key as a quoted string, regardless of how it's
' written in source — but a hook body like this one is ordinary BrightScript-shaped code the
' compiler does not rewrite, so the bareword-key pitfall applies here exactly like it would in
' hand-written BrightScript. Any key that must match a real HTTP query param/header name
' case-exactly (as most APIs require) should be quoted for this reason — see findings/requests-runtime.md.
private function buildRequest(requestData: object): object {
  return { query: { "userId": requestData.userId } }
}

' parseResponse/parseError are `private function` here — safe because the compiler calls them by
' their compiled name (`private_parseResponse`), not a bare string, unlike `observeFieldScoped`'s
' target in RequestDemoScreen.thr (see that file's own comment on the distinction).
'
' response.data is the parsed JSON array (ft_httpFetch parses a non-empty response body as JSON
' when it's valid JSON — see runtime-assets/Http/FlashTheaterHttp.brs) — reshaped here into just
' what its two callers actually use: RequestDemoScreen.thr only ever reads `count`, while
' RouterTransitionDemo.thr also renders `posts` as a real, dynamically-created, focusable
' `{#each}` list (trimmed to `id`/`title` — the only two fields either caller needs, not the whole
' per-post payload jsonplaceholder returns).
private function parseResponse(response: object): object {
  posts = []
  for (i = 0 to response.data.Count() - 1) {
    item = response.data[i]
    posts.Push({ id: item.id, title: item.title })
  }
  return { count: response.data.Count(), posts: posts }
}

private function parseError(response: object): object {
  return { message: "HTTP " + response.httpStatusCode.ToStr() }
}
</script>

<component extends="Task">
</component>
